{"id":"h069","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"claim","question":"Is the following claim true? The SI base unit of luminous intensity is the candela.","truth":"yes","model_answer":"yes","correct":true,"source":"author-written for this project; verified by the author","claims":[{"text":"The SI has seven base units covering time, length, mass, current, temperature, amount of substance, and luminous intensity.","confidence":0.98,"lean":0.8,"theta":1.5394,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The candela is the SI base unit of luminous intensity.","confidence":0.99,"lean":1,"theta":1.5551,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The candela is defined via monochromatic radiation at 540 THz with radiant intensity of 1/683 W per steradian.","confidence":0.95,"lean":0.6,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"This definition has been in the SI since 1979 and remains current.","confidence":0.85,"lean":0.5,"theta":1.3352,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The lumen, lux, and nit are derived photometric units expressed in terms of the candela.","confidence":0.95,"lean":0.5,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Confusion with derived photometric units is the only real source of doubt about the claim.","confidence":0.8,"lean":-0.2,"theta":1.2566,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Derived photometric units like lumen, lux, and nit are not base units and so do not contradict the claim.","confidence":0.95,"lean":0.8,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The SI definition itself settles the question.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":3,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":4,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":6,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":7,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":2,"j":3,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":2,